    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Homeowners insurance in Utah covers a roof leak only if it results from a sudden, accidental event\u2014such as a windstorm, hailstorm, or fallen tree. Insurance will not cover leaks caused by normal wear and tear, old age, poor maintenance, or long-term water seepage .",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Homeowners insurance in Utah covers a roof leak only if it results from a sudden, accidental event\u2014such as a windstorm, hailstorm, or fallen tree"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What Is Covered"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Sudden Storm Damage: Leaks caused by severe wind, heavy hail, or lightning that puncture or damage the roofing material.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Falling Objects: Damage from trees, branches, or flying debris during a storm.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Interior Water Damage: If the roof breach comes from a covered storm event, the policy may also pay to repair damaged interior drywall, insulation, and flooring.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What Is Not Covered"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Wear and Tear: Leaks from old or deteriorating shingles, aging roofs (typically 20+ years old), or general material breakdown.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Lack of Maintenance: Issues stemming from ignored missing shingles, clogged gutters, or unsealed flashing.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Flooding: Rainwater backing up from the ground or rising water requires a separate flood policy through the National Flood Insurance Program rather than standard home insurance.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "National Flood Insurance Program",
                "link": "https://www.ksl.com/article/news/where-insurance-can-and-cant-help-with-storm-damage/51578726"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Steps to Take If You Have a Leak"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Tarp the Roof: Temporarily cover the active leak to prevent further interior damage, but avoid major permanent repairs before the adjuster visits.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              8,
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Document Everything: Take clear photos and videos of the interior water damage and the roof exterior, and keep a record using guidance from a local source like Fullstack Roofing .",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "Fullstack Roofing",
                "link": "https://fullstackroofing.com/services/insurance-claims"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Get a Professional Inspection: Have a licensed roofer evaluate the deck, underlayment, and flashing to pinpoint the exact cause before calling your carrier.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              10
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "File the Claim Promptly: Contact your insurance company. Note that Utah law or policy terms generally give you a limited window (often 1 to 3 years depending on the carrier and circumstances) to file storm-related claims.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              11,
              12
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow down your options, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What caused the leak (a recent storm, old age, etc.)?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "How old is your roof?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
